{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Read the isolated-network receipt (e3bd14fe94534fbca3955cae4e521df7, completed, tests_passed, exit 0, no changed target files) and recorded the revision for the read-index growth account. Both comparisons hold with complete admissions: index 0 is the admission (leader 1 of a real three-node network in StateLeader with committed 1 and committedEntryInCurrentTerm true, CheckQuorum false, ReadOnlySafe) and index 1 is the probe showing 32 same-context ReadIndex requests admitted as unconfirmed 32 with confirmed 0 while the leader is isolated from both followers, plus 64 heartbeat messages. Two things change: the trigger is now exercised through a real network configuration rather than only by withholding responses, and the amplification figure is corrected upward from one heartbeat per retry to one per peer per retry, which is 64 for 32 retries with two peers. I recorded a measured fact tied to index 1 and revised the interpretation, keeping the defect claim asserted and the conditions otherwise unchanged: ReadOnlySafe, a leader unable to obtain quorum acknowledgements, CheckQuorum at its default of false, and a caller retrying per the documented pattern; under those conditions the library retains one request per retry with no collapsing and no reclamation while the process lives, pins caller-controlled payload, emits one heartbeat per peer per retry, and releases the queue as a per-entry, per-peer burst. Severity remains measured only for retained payload and outbound heartbeats, with footprint, OOM and transport saturation explicitly unclaimed, and the partition modelled by isolation rather than a real transport.\"}"}}
